OpenStreetMap - Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0
------------------------------------------------

    data/places.json        339 place names with coordinates

    Coordinates resolved through OpenStreetMap's Nominatim service and shipped
    so that a unit with no network can still name what an antenna reaches.

        (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
        Made available under the Open Database License,
        https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/

    The place names themselves are ordinary knowledge; the coordinates came
    from OSM, so the ODbL's attribution and share-alike terms apply to that
    file and to any database derived from it. tools/build_places.py is the
    script that produced it.


Fetched at runtime, not shipped
-------------------------------

    These are looked up by the running program and cached under data/. They
    are nobody's to redistribute here, and are named so that anyone auditing
    an installation knows where its cached files came from.

    OpenStreetMap Nominatim and Overpass  - place lookups and neighbours
        (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL 1.0, as above.
        Cached under data/geocode/ and data/places/.

    OpenTopoData, SRTM 30 m               - ground profiles for path analysis
        SRTM is a NASA/USGS product in the public domain; the service is a
        courtesy, used within its published rate limits.
        Cached under data/terrain/.

    CISA NIFOG                            - interoperability channels
        A work of the US government, no copyright, freely reproducible.
        CISA's to revise, so ELMER keeps a copy rather than a fork.
        Cached under data/nifog/.

    Repeater data                         - read, never redistributed
        data/repeaters.json is built on this machine from whatever the
        operator already holds - a TowerWitch installation, or their own
        RepeaterBook export. It is gitignored and ships with nothing. ELMER
        does no repeater lookups of its own, deliberately: that data belongs
        to whoever holds the subscription it came from, and this project has
        no licence to pass it on.


Why the pools are here at all
-----------------------------

Both bodies publish these pools so that anyone can study and teach from them,
and ELMER reproduces the questions and keyed answers exactly as released,
because that is what appears on the test. Redistributing them is intended and
permitted; relicensing them is not, which is why the LICENSE is scoped and
this file exists.
